course: dictionary
lesson: 6845
title: Outrage: anger that spreads
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Outrage is a big feeling. It is anger mixed with shock, and it comes when something seems deeply unfair or wrong. «Some talk radio and TV shows stir up outrage on purpose, because angry listeners keep listening.» Stir up means make it grow, so the shows make people angry and shocked on purpose.
explain: Look at the clues in the sentence. The words «on purpose» tell you the shows do this deliberately, and «angry listeners keep listening» shows that the anger is what keeps people watching. So outrage is strong anger, not just a small annoyance. Try the swap: "stir up anger and shock on purpose" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Outrage can also be a verb. To outrage people is to make them feel very angry and shocked. People can be outraged by something, as in «She was outraged by the unfair rule.» Often in everyday talk someone says "It's an outrage!" about something small, as a joke. The word is strong, so save it for things that really matter when you write.
fact: Outrage is a noun meaning a very strong feeling of anger and shock.
fact: Outrage is also a verb: to outrage people means to make them very angry and shocked.
fact: «The attack was an outrage.» Here outrage means an act so cruel or wrong that it makes people very angry.
fact: Phrases to know: public outrage, cause outrage, express outrage, be outraged by.
fact: Outrage often shows up in the news, and people may say "It's an outrage!" jokingly about small things.
exercise: «Some talk radio and TV shows stir up outrage on purpose, because angry listeners keep listening.» What does outrage mean here? | Happy cheering for the shows | *Strong anger and shock at something unfair or wrong | A new kind of television | A short, quiet pause in the programme
exercise: «The attack was an outrage.» What does outrage mean in this sentence? | A fast game played outdoors | A small mistake that nobody notices | A kind and gentle action | *An act so cruel or wrong that it makes people very angry
exercise: Ella reads about public outrage over an unfair rule. What does public outrage mean? | *Anger and shock felt by many people | A quiet park with no people | Tiredness after a long day | A shop that sells paper
test: To outrage people means | to make them feel hungry | to make them feel bored | *to make them feel very angry and shocked | to make them feel calm and safe
test: «She was outraged by the unfair rule.» How did she feel? | *Very angry and shocked | Delighted and proud | Calm and relaxed | Bored and sleepy
exam: Why do some talk radio shows stir up outrage on purpose? | *Because angry listeners keep listening | Because they want to sell cakes | Because they are sleepy | Because the weather is bad
